{"id":"6a0f4186-84e3-4e3c-a006-d3719a1b165b","object":"chat.completion","created":1795243411,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"SELECT t.order_month, ROUND(AVG(t.order_amount), 2) AS avg_order_amount\nFROM (\n  SELECT o.order_id,\n         DATE_TRUNC('month', o.order_purchase_timestamp) AS order_month,\n         SUM(oi.price) AS order_amount\n  FROM orders o\n  JOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id = o.order_id\n  GROUP BY o.order_id, o.order_purchase_timestamp\n) t\nGROUP BY t.order_month\nORDER BY t.order_month\nLIMIT 1000;","reasoning_content":"The error is \"bad SQL grammar\" — likely the alias \"month\" being used in GROUP BY... Actually PostgreSQL allows GROUP BY alias. Hmm, \"month\" is not reserved. Actually the issue might be `ORDER BY month` where month... no.\n\nPossibly the issue is the outer query GROUP BY month referencing output alias — that's allowed in PostgreSQL. Hmm.\n\nWait — maybe the issue is that DATE_TRUNC returns timestamp without time zone, fine.\n\nActually maybe the problem is the subquery alias `t` ... no.\n\nCould it be that the JDBC prepares statement and the parser... \"bad SQL grammar\" is a Spring exception wrapping. Perhaps DATE_TRUNC('month', ...) is fine.\n\nHmm, maybe \"month\" is a reserved-ish word in some contexts? In PostgreSQL, MONTH is a non-reserved keyword. Actually `month` as a column alias is fine.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the issue is `ROUND(AVG(t.order_amount), 2)` — AVG returns numeric for numeric input, so ROUND with two args is fine.\n\nHmm, maybe LIMIT 1000 — fine.\n\nAnother thought: the parser might not handle multi-line? Unlikely.\n\nMaybe the real issue: GROUP BY month — in outer query, alias \"month\" — PostgreSQL resolves GROUP BY to input column names first; t has column \"month\" so it's fine either way.\n\nPossibly the platform's SQL validator rejects subqueries? But error is bad SQL grammar from PreparedStatementCallback.\n\nMaybe safe approach: rewrite without subquery using two levels but simpler: avoid alias named month, use group by 1, and avoid DATE_TRUNC duplicated. Let's write:\n\nSELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', o.order_purchase_timestamp) AS month_start, ROUND(AVG(oi_price_sum),2) ...\n\nLet me just rewrite avoiding the alias \"month\" and using GROUP BY 1, ORDER BY 1.\n\nNew SQL:\n\nSELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', o.order_purchase_timestamp) AS month_start,\n       ROUND(AVG(t.order_amount), 2) AS avg_order_amount\nFROM (\n  SELECT o.order_id, MIN(o.order_purchase_timestamp) AS purchase_ts, SUM(oi.price) AS order_amount\n  FROM orders o\n  JOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id = o.order_id\n  GROUP BY o.order_id\n) t\nJOIN orders o ON o.order_id = t.order_id  -- ugly\n\nBetter: keep month in subquery but rename alias to order_month.\n\nSELECT t.order_month, ROUND(AVG(t.order_amount),2) AS avg_order_amount\nFROM (SELECT o.order_id, DATE_TRUNC('month', o.order_purchase_timestamp) AS order_month, SUM(oi.price) AS order_amount\n      FROM orders o JOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id = o.order_id\n      GROUP BY o.order_id, o.order_purchase_timestamp) t\nGROUP BY t.order_month ORDER BY t.order_month LIMIT 1000;\n\nGrouping by o.order_purchase_timestamp works since order_id is PK.\n\nHmm but DATE_TRUNC returns timestamp; ordering fine.\n\nMaybe the actual grammar error is because of the alias `month`? To be safe use order_month everywhere and qualify in GROUP BY.\n\nActually wait — maybe the issue: \"month\" — in some strict parsers it's fine. Let's just avoid.\n\nAlso possible the grammar error came from something else: 'month' inside DATE_TRUNC as string is fine.\n\nI'll output the rewritten SQL."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":3536,"completion_tokens":857,"total_tokens":4393,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2944},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":743},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2944,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":592},"system_fingerprint":"bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb157